<header><p class="enc-kicker">State</p><h1 class="enc-title">Mali Federation</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1959">1959</a>–<a href="/years/1960">1960</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/20th-century">20th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mali">Mali</a> Federation was a union of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/senegal">Senegal</a> and the Sudanese Republic, the former French <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sudan">Sudan</a>, formed in 1959 within the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/french-community">French Community</a>. It became independent on 20 June 1960 with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dakar-senegal">Dakar</a> as capital, but disputes between <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/leopold-sedar-senghor">Léopold Sédar Senghor</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/modibo-keita">Modibo Keïta</a> led Senegal to withdraw in August 1960. The Sudanese Republic then proclaimed itself the Republic of Mali.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/mali-federation.jpg" alt="Mali Federation" width="320" height="213" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: SKopp · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Flag_of_Mali_(1959%E2%80%931961).svg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Origins</h2><p>After the May 1958 crisis the colonies of French West Africa were offered a choice between immediate independence and membership of a reorganised <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/french-community">French Community</a>, and only <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/guinea">Guinea</a> chose independence. In late November 1958 French Sudan, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/senegal">Senegal</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/upper-volta">Upper Volta</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kingdom-of-dahomey">Dahomey</a> declared their intention to form a federation. A congress at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bamako">Bamako</a> from 28 to 30 December, with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/modibo-keita">Modibo Keïta</a> as its president, produced the name Mali Federation, largely through <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/leopold-sedar-senghor">Léopold Sédar Senghor</a>. Pressure from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/france">France</a> and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ivory-coast">Ivory Coast</a> kept Upper Volta and Dahomey from ratifying the constitution, so only French Sudan and Senegal remained.</p><p>The federation was to rest on parity: a federal assembly of 20 members from each colony, six ministers, three from each, and a single party. Until a president was elected, Keïta was premier and Mamadou Dia of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/senegal">Senegal</a> vice-premier in charge of the armed forces. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/france">France</a>, which had resisted the federation, supported it once both countries agreed to remain in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/french-community">French Community</a> and keep French military bases.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p>Tensions grew over the powers of a federal executive: French <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sudan">Sudan</a> wanted a single strong executive, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/senegal">Senegal</a> wanted to keep parity. Senghor and Keïta also had different visions, Keïta claiming later that he pursued socialism and Senghor a bourgeois agenda. The crisis came in August 1960, as a federal president was to be elected, and on 19 August Keïta dismissed Dia as defence minister, declared a state of emergency and mobilised the army. Senghor and Dia had the army demobilised and called out the gendarmerie, which surrounded Keïta's house and the government offices. Senegal declared its independence from the federation at a midnight session on 20 August. Little violence occurred, and the Sudanese officials were sent back to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bamako">Bamako</a> on a sealed train.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Legacy</h2><p>On 22 September 1960 Keïta's party renamed the Sudanese Republic Mali and broke its ties with the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/french-community">French Community</a>. Both countries were admitted to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/united-nations">United Nations</a> later that month. Senghor was president of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/senegal">Senegal</a> from 1960 to 1980 and Keïta of Mali from 1960 to 1968. Keïta refused diplomatic relations with Senegal for many years, and Senghor became wary of unification efforts. The railway between the two countries reopened on 22 June 1963, when the two leaders embraced at the border.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Mali Federation” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Senegal and French Sudan briefly formed the <b>Mali Federation</b>, which broke apart in August 1960, and by the end of 1960 all the former territories of French West Africa were independent states.</blockquote><a href="/empire/french-west-africa">French West Africa · Decline &amp; Fall</a></li><li><blockquote>The union of Ghana and Guinea, later joined by Mali, remained largely on paper and lapsed in 1963; the <b>Mali Federation</b> of Senegal and French Sudan broke apart in 1960 within months of independence; and the East African Community of Kenya, Uganda and Tanzania collapsed in 1977.</blockquote><a href="/movement/pan-africanism">Pan-Africanism · Outcomes</a></li><li><blockquote>Senegal became an autonomous republic within the French Community in 1958, joined the <b>Mali Federation</b> in 1959, and became fully independent on 20 August 1960 under Leopold Sedar Senghor.</blockquote><a href="/state/french-senegal">French Senegal</a></li><li><blockquote>It joined Senegal in the <b>Mali Federation</b> in 1959; after Senegal withdrew, it declared independence as Mali on 22 September 1960.</blockquote><a href="/state/french-sudan">French Sudan</a></li><li><blockquote>After the French Sudan and Senegal formed the <b>Mali Federation</b>, which became independent on 20 June 1960, Senegal withdrew and the French Sudan was renamed the Republic of Mali.</blockquote><a href="/state/mali">Mali</a></li><li><blockquote>French Senegal joined the French Sudan in January 1959 to form the <b>Mali Federation</b>, which became fully independent on 20 June 1960.</blockquote><a href="/state/senegal">Senegal</a></li></ul></section>
